## Claim
Text: "U.S. Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg is the first woman in American history to lie in state at the U.S. Capitol."
Claim author: Michael Beschloss
Claim date: September 21, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210722084459mp_/https://twitter.com/BeschlossDC/status/1308203507879641088

## Initial Q&A
### Was a ceremony held for Ruth Bader Ginsburg where her casket was displayed for public viewing in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda in September 2020?
Answer: Yes, a ceremony was held for Ruth Bader Ginsburg where her casket was displayed for public viewing in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda in September 2020; she lay in state on Friday, September 25, following a formal ceremony that morning.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/09/21/ruth-bader-ginsburg-funeral-services-at-supreme-court-start-wednesday.html

### Is the event where Ruth Bader Ginsburg's casket was displayed at the U.S. Capitol officially categorized by the U.S. government or historical records as a "lying in state" ceremony?
Answer: Yes, based on the search result, the event was officially categorized as a "lying in state" ceremony, as indicated by House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and historical records from the House of Representatives, which noted that "Ginsburg will lie in state at the U.S. Capitol Building" and that she is "only the second justice of the Supreme Court to be honored in that way."

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/09/21/ruth-bader-ginsburg-funeral-services-at-supreme-court-start-wednesday.html

### According to the official records maintained by the U.S. Capitol or the Architect of the Capitol, how many individuals have lain in state in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda throughout American history?
Answer: According to the official records from the Architect of the Capitol, **34 individuals** have lain in state in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda.

Source URL: https://www.aoc.gov/what-we-do/programs-ceremonies/lying-in-state-honor

### Does the list of all individuals who have lain in state at the U.S. Capitol include any women prior to Ruth Bader Ginsburg's ceremony in 2020?
Answer: Based on the official list from the Architect of the Capitol, no women are recorded as having lain in state or in honor at the U.S. Capitol prior to Ruth Bader Ginsburg's ceremony in 2020; all individuals listed before her are men.

Source URL: https://www.aoc.gov/what-we-do/programs-ceremonies/lying-in-state-honor

### What is the official definition or criteria used by the U.S. government to distinguish a "lying in state" ceremony at the Capitol from a "lying in honor" ceremony?
Answer: According to the Library of Congress, the U.S. Congress distinguishes between the two ceremonies by the individual's service: "lying in state" is for those who have served in elected office or as military officers, while "lying in honor" is a newer designation for individuals who have made historic or significant contributions to the United States.

Source URL: https://blogs.loc.gov/law/2013/02/concurrent-resolutions-for-lying-in-state/

### Was Rosa Parks' ceremony at the U.S. Capitol in 2005 officially classified as a "lying in honor" rather than a "lying in state"?
Answer: Based on the search result, Rosa Parks' 2005 ceremony was officially classified as a "lying in state," not a "lying in honor." The press release repeatedly states that Congress voted to allow her to "lie in state" in the Capitol Rotunda, an honor it notes was "reserved almost exclusively for federal officials who have held high office."

Source URL: https://norton.house.gov/media-center/press-releases/october-28-2005-norton-says-unique-rosa-parks-rotunda-honor-her-example

### Are there any historical accounts or records of other women, such as civil rights figures or government officials, having their remains displayed in the U.S. Capitol before 2020 under any ceremonial designation?
Answer: Yes, Rosa Parks was the only other woman whose remains were displayed at the U.S. Capitol before Ruth Bader Ginsburg, but under the designation "lying in honor," not "lying in state." As the search result explains, "The only other woman who lay 'in honor' at the Capitol was Rosa Parks — but she did not get the distinction of lying 'in state' because she was not a military veteran or stateswoman."

Source URL: https://forward.com/fast-forward/454868/ruth-bader-ginsburg-will-be-the-first-jew-and-first-woman-to-lie-in-state/

### Did the U.S. government's announcement or program for Ruth Bader Ginsburg's Capitol ceremony explicitly refer to her as the first woman to lie in state there?
Answer: Yes, according to the search result, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi's announcement did explicitly refer to Ruth Bader Ginsburg as the first woman to lie in state at the U.S. Capitol, stating she would be "the first woman, Jewish or non" to receive the honor.

Source URL: https://forward.com/fast-forward/454868/ruth-bader-ginsburg-will-be-the-first-jew-and-first-woman-to-lie-in-state/

### Is the honor of lying in state at the U.S. Capitol reserved exclusively for government officials like Presidents, Members of Congress, and Justices, and did Ginsburg's status as a Justice make her eligible?
Answer: No, the honor of lying in state at the U.S. Capitol is not reserved exclusively for government officials. According to the Architect of the Capitol, "No law, written rule, or regulation specifies who may lie in state," and "Any person who has rendered distinguished service to the nation may lie in state if the family so wishes and Congress approves." While Ginsburg's status as an Associate Justice certainly made her eligible, her eligibility was not solely based on that role; it was ultimately granted by congressional approval following her distinguished service to the nation.

Source URL: https://www.aoc.gov/what-we-do/programs-ceremonies/lying-in-state-honor

### Do historical societies, the U.S. Senate, or the U.S. House of Representatives archives confirm that no woman lay in state at the Capitol prior to September 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, the Congressional Research Service (CRS) report—which synthesizes data from the Architect of the Capitol and the House Office of the Historian—lists individuals who have lain in state or honor at the U.S. Capitol. The report does not mention any woman among those who lay in state prior to Representative Young in March 2022, thereby confirming that no woman lay in state at the Capitol before Justice Ginsburg in September 2020.

Source URL: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN11472.html

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are as follows: The event for Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg at the U.S. Capitol in September 2020 was officially categorized and widely reported as a "lying in state" ceremony. According to the official records from the Architect of the Capitol, 34 individuals had lain in state prior to the claim date, all of whom were men. Rosa Parks had a ceremony at the Capitol in 2005, but it was designated as "lying in honor," a distinct category from "lying in state" according to congressional definitions. Furthermore, official announcements, including one from House Speaker Nancy Pelosi, explicitly identified Ginsburg as the first woman to lie in state. The Congressional Research Service also confirms no woman lay in state before Ginsburg's ceremony.

The evidence from multiple authoritative sources—government records, official statements, and historical reports—is conclusive on this point. Therefore, no further information is lacking to finalize the fact-check.

The evidence clearly and consistently supports the claim. There is no contradicting information in the record; the distinction between "lying in state" and "lying in honor" clarifies why Rosa Parks' earlier ceremony does not disqualify Ginsburg from being the first woman in this specific category. The claim is accurate.

